{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792314613,"nonce":"UduvJRD/tN6MNvbERm/kvWt1d9M=","title":"A page that lays the canvas out itself cannot keep the program from resizing it","message":"The window agent turns the program's requests for the window's size, minimum and maximum into the canvas's inline CSS. A page that makes the canvas fill the window then has to beat inline style (with `!important`) to stop a program that asks for 1280 by 820 from fixing the canvas at that size.\n\nThe user's direction: hlwindow should detect the canvas's dimension, not take the program's.\n\n**Fix**\n\nA canvas marked `data-xwindow=\"fit\"` is the window's size whatever the program asks of it: the agent leaves its CSS alone, and it posts the size the page lays out, so the window is what the page makes it, at the page's scale, followed as it changes. A canvas without the mark behaves as before.","files":null}]}